From 84149f2ac4c223c5b17f4643dbdd17c11ba4b5bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Larry Gritz <lg@larrygritz.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 15:41:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(ImageInput): Reject absurd tile/display sizes, and
 related fixes

Fixes #5506.

ImageInput::check_open() applied "limits:resolution" only to the data
window, so a tiny EXR declaring, for example, a 2^28-wide tile or an
838M-wide display window got through, and later read_tiles() or maketx
(which pads out to the full window) tried to allocate terabytes. Now
also apply "limits:resolution" to the full/display window and tile
dimensions, and "limits:imagesize_MB" to a single tile.

Extras found along the way:

Reshuffle the order of checks in check_open, so that the deterministic
checks (like resolution) are first, and the "limits:imagesize_MB"
checks (which depends on physical memory) are last. This helps ensure
that a corrupt file fails the same way on every machine.

When an ImageBuf allocation did fail, the ImageBuf ctor and reset()
went on to zero() the missing pixels (assert/segfault), and IBA
operations wrote through a LOCALBUFFER ImageBuf with no memory. Skip the
zeroing when there are no pixels, make IBAprep fail with an error for
such a dst, and have maketx check the top-level allocation.

Have runtest.py pin limits:imagesize_MB=32768 via OPENIMAGEIO_OPTIONS
unless already set, so the "bomb" tests exercise the format-specific
compression-ratio checks rather than tripping the memory-dependent
size limit on low-memory GHA CI runners. Make openexr-copy's
openexr:core=1 detection tolerate extra options.

Assisted-by: Claude Code / Claude Opus 5.5

Signed-off-by: Larry Gritz <lg@larrygritz.com>
